{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"reasoning","text":"Now it's clear:\n- Raw counts (integers), need normalization. Library sizes differ → CPM 1e4 + log1p per dataset.\n- Reference: 43 guides = 10 NT controls (NT-I1..I10) + 33 perturbed guides, one guide per perturbed gene. Target genes = 33 perturbed + NT = 34.\n- Query: 42 guides = 10 NT (NT-I1..I10, same names) + 32 anonymized perturbed guides (guide1..guide32).\n\nSo the query has 32 anonymized perturbed guides against the reference's 33 perturbed genes. One reference gene has no query counterpart (or one of the query's guides is something else). Therefore, per-target_gene row mapping: 34 rows; one gene gets assigned a wrong/least-bad query guide (its true guide isn't in the query). This is where the ambiguity/confidence matters — that gene should have a low confidence.\n\nHungarian method: 34 reference genes vs 42 query guides — each reference gene gets assigned a unique query guide; 8 query guides unassigned. The missing gene will steal someone else's guide... Hungarian maximizes the sum, so it will assign the missing gene the best guide among those available. That gene's confidence (margin) should be relatively low. OK.\n\nWait, actually — if there are 32 perturbed query guides and 33 perturbed reference genes, then the query has one fewer perturbed gene. Also, the 10 query NT guides match the 10 reference NT guides by name — but we must not match by name (leakage via guide-name matching). Signature-based matching should map NT guides to NT. For the NT gene row: with 10 NT query guides and 1 NT reference row, the NT row gets assigned the best-correlating NT guide. The other 9 NT query guides remain unassigned. No problem.\n\nCell type shift: let me quickly check whether the query comes from a different cell type — e.g., correlate NT means. Actually not needed; the method is robust either way. But in the report I should characterize the shift. A single lightweight characterization is fine: