NT row got an NT guide... so perturbed query guides: 32 available; 32 assigned to the 33 perturbed genes minus... wait: total 34 rows: NT + 33 genes. Query guides: 10 NT + 32 perturbed = 42. Hungarian assigns each of the 34 rows a unique guide. PHF3→NT-I7, SCAF8→NT-I3, NT→NT-I8. So perturbed guides assigned to 31 of the 33 perturbed genes; 2 perturbed genes got nothing (i.e., their true guides missing from query? no — only one gene's guide is missing from the query).\n\nHmm wait: 32 perturbed query guides vs 33 perturbed reference genes → exactly one perturbed reference gene has no counterpart. Hungarian left 2 perturbed genes unassigned to perturbed guides (PHF3, SCAF8 got NT guides) and gave one perturbed query guide to... let me count: assigned perturbed guides = 32 genes? Genes assigned guide1-32: all except PHF3 and SCAF8 → 31 genes using 31 perturbed guides; one perturbed query guide remains unassigned. Wait, there are 34 rows: NT (NT-I8), PHF3 (NT-I7), SCAF8 (NT-I3), and the remaining 31 genes use 31 of the 32 perturbed guides. One perturbed guide left over. Hmm, this suggests that one of the 31 assignments is \"wrong\" (stealing someone else's guide), and both PHF3 and SCAF8 lack a guide — impossible, since only one gene lacks a query counterpart... unless one of PHF3/SCAF8's true counterparts in the query exists but was stolen due to weak correlation.\n\nAlso, subsample stability is 0.76 — 8 out of 34 rows changed under 50% subsampling. Many margins are negative (runner-up beats the assigned guide). This is because Hungarian forces global one-to-one, and the correlation structure here is noisy: perturbation signatures between the two datasets correlate weakly (0.1–0.67). Cross-dataset perturbation signature correlations for the same gene are usually decent (>0.5 for strong knockdowns), but many here are low — suggesting there's a substantial cell type shift between reference and query (ctrl_profile_corr=0.918, not ~0.99). So the \"cell type shift\" is real: