thway; LEO1 is in PAF1 complex / transcription elongation). Hmm, this is weak.\n\nA better validation approach: leave-one-out internal consistency. For each reference gene, we have exactly one guide — no replication within reference.\n\nAlternative strong validation: the query's NT controls. There are 10 of them. Their signatures should be pure noise (≈0). Any assignment of a perturbed guide to a gene should correlate poorly with NT query guides. Already used.\n\nHonestly, the cleanest lightweight validation is subsample stability (done) + margin statistics. And report ambiguity honestly. But before finalizing, let me verify the raw C matrix values for the 3 genes of interest to see how clear the signal is, and check the directionality issue: rows with negative margins (runner-up beats assigned) — the report must state that global one-to-one assignment forces some genes to take suboptimal guides.\n\nHmm wait. Actually let me reconsider whether Hungarian one-to-one is the right choice. If query has 32 perturbed guides and reference has 33 perturbed genes, and each query guide targets exactly one of 33 genes (one gene missing), then one-to-one is correct. The PHF3/SCAF8 situation suggests one of them is the missing gene and the other's assignment is confused. Which one is missing? Check: SCAF8's best correlation is only 0.054 (noise level), PHF3's best is 0.066 (noise level). Both look like they have no counterpart! But there's only one missing slot...\n\nUnless the query doesn't drop any gene but the signatures are genuinely noisy across the cell type shift. Let me look at the top correlations per query guide from the supplementary CSV to see the overall picture.\n\nAlso let me reconsider: the normalization/shift issue. ctrl_profile_corr=0.918 between reference NT mean and query NT mean — moderate shift. Differencing handles baseline, but if cell type composition within NT differs... well, fine.\n\nPerhaps a better primary similarity metric: Pearson on signatures res