{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_26","type":"reasoning","text":"Now the situation is clearer. Observations:\n\n1. NT query guides: best correlations are all ~0.01–0.10 (noise level) — good. These don't contain true perturbation signal, and none of them are strongly assigned to any gene.\n\n2. guide32: best correlation is 0.0516 (RPRD1B) — noise level. So guide32 looks like it has no true signal either?? Or it's the query guide corresponding to the missing gene, but… no wait. Hmm, guide32's best is 0.05 — essentially noise. So either guide32 targets a gene whose reference signature transfers poorly, or it's a failed/weak perturbation.\n\n3. SCAF1 has many guides (guide1, 11, 15, 16, 23, 26, 27 → 7 guides!) claiming it as their best. That's suspicious — the reference SCAF1 signature is acting as an attractor. This suggests the SCAF1 reference signature is generic (a shared stress/differentiation response?) and many query guides correlate with it.\n\n4. CPSF1/CPSF4 attract guide10, 12, 17, 22, 25, 29, 3, 4, 8 — highly correlated signatures between CPSF1 and CPSF4 (runner_up scores are almost identical to the scores). These are subunits of the same complex (cleavage factor) — the KD signatures are genuinely similar (same pathway), so ambiguity between CPSF1/CPSF4/CPSF2/CPSF3 is biological reality.\n\nThe cross-dataset signature correlations are overall weak (0.2–0.67). Cell-type shifts substantially degrade the transfer. The task explicitly acknowledges this: \"across the cell-type shift … quantify the ambiguity.\"\n\nNow, are PABPC1→guide18, NUDT21→guide13, LEO1→guide14 correct?\n- guide18: best=PABPC1 0.532, runner-up SCAF1 0.376 — clear winner for PABPC1.\n- guide13: best=NUDT21 0.636, runner-up CPSF6 0.526 — NUDT21 and CPSF6 are in the same 3′ processing pathway, so plausible.\n- guide14: reference LEO1's best query guide = guide14 (0.314, runner-up 0.223) — but from the query side, guide14's best is CTR9 (0.370), with LEO1 not even in the top 2 (runner-up SCAF1 0.364)!\n\nContradiction for LEO1/CTR9/guide14: reference CTR9's best query guide is guide28 (0.3096, margin −0.06), and reference LEO1 gets guide14 (0.314). But guide14's own best reference gene is CTR9 (0.370). And guide28's best reference gene is CSTF1 (0.371)! So the LEO1–CTR9 region is thoroughly ambiguous: CTR9, LEO1, CSTF1, SCAF1 signatures mutually correlate at 0.3–0.37. Both CTR9 and LEO1 are subunits of the PAF1 complex — KD signatures should look similar. This is genuine biological ambiguity.\n\nHmm, wait: is it possible that guide14 targets CTR9 and guide28 targets LEO1? Or guide14→LEO1, guide28→CTR9? The Hungarian method assigned LEO1→guide14 (because the CTR9 row chose guide28). Correlations: C[CTR9, guide14]=0.3698, C[CTR9, guide28]=0.3096, C[LEO1, guide14]=0.3140, C[LEO1, guide28]=? Unknown. Hungarian assignment: CTR9→guide28 (0.3096), LEO1→guide14 (0.3140). Sum = 0.6236. Alternative: CTR9→guide14 (0.3698), LEO1→guide28 (C[LEO1,guide28]=?) — this only wins if C[LEO1,guide28] > 0.2538. Probably it was lower.\n\nThis is exactly the kind of ambiguity that should be quantified. The PAF1 complex genes (CTR9, LEO1, PAF1, CDC73) have similar signatures.\n\nOK, but here's my concern: with this much noise, is my primary method good enough? Options for improving robustness while staying within \"one primary method\":\n- Use Spearman instead of Pearson (rank-based, more robust to cell-type shifts).\n- Restrict to informative genes (e.g., exclude genes with near-zero variance in the signature, or use top genes by reference signature variance).\n\nBut these change the method. Task instructions say start simple and quantify ambiguity. My current results may be acceptable: the three genes of interest have reasonable assignments with quantified confidence/margin, and the report documents the ambiguity (negative margin, subsample stability 76%).\n\nBut wait — subsample stability 0.76 means 8 rows flip when cells are halved. That's weak. And NT sanity check 0.0 (as designed) — my check was poorly designed: the NT signature is a noise vector, so \"best hit is NT\" is not expected. I should redefine NT sanity: the NT row is assigned an NT guide (TRUE), and NT query guides don't show strong signal (max correlation across all genes ≤0.10) (confirmed: max is 0.1027 for NT-I5). This is a valid sanity result: controls have no signal, confirming that signature correlations capture perturbation biology rather than technical artifacts.\n\nLet me reconsider the primary method once more. There's a more powerful standard approach for this kind of cross-context Perturb-seq mapping: instead of correlating raw logFC signatures across all genes, correlate on the top genes that discriminate reference guides (high-variance signature genes), or use rank correlation. The overall weak correlations (0.1–0.6) suggest cell-type shifts are changing many effect sizes.\n\nHmm, but actually let me sanity-check a key assumption: is the query really a different cell type, or the same cell type in a different state? ctrl_profile_corr=0.918. Let me quickly check: the correlation of NT means is 0.918, but what about the correlations of individual perturbation signatures across datasets? For example, the diagonal (true pairs, if my mapping is right): CPSF1→guide4 at 0.67, NUDT21→guide13 at 0.64, PABPC1→guide18 at 0.53. Off-diagonals are typically 0.1–0.4. There is signal.\n\nI think the method is reasonable; but let me quickly evaluate whether Spearman gives meaningfully different results for the 3 genes of interest — as a validation check (robustness of the final answer to the similarity metric). If all 3 genes agree across Pearson/Spearman, confidence goes up. This is a legitimate lightweight validation of the answer, not a third method or a sensitivity-analysis expansion… borderline. The instructions say one primary method + one lightweight independent validation, no method comparison. Subsample stability is my validation. Adding Spearman comparison risks violating \"no third-method comparison.\" Skip Spearman; keep subsample stability + NT control sanity.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider the row semantics of the deliverable once more. Rows = 34 (including NT). Hmm, one more consideration: maybe the intended rows are just the 33 perturbed genes? Or 42 query guides? I chose the 34 reference target_genes. The schema has \"target_gene\" first, and the task says \"identify the query guide IDs corresponding to PABPC1, NUDT21, LEO1\" — a per-reference-gene row gives that directly. Also, the supplementary file covers the per-query-guide view. I'm confident in the 34-row choice.\n\nAlso, \"confidence\" as currently defined is a softmax over 42 guides — values around 0.03 (with 42 candidates, chance is ~0.024). The spread is small. That's honest but maybe not very informative. It's fine — documented. Should I use a better confidence definition: margin-based logistic? Keep softmax, document it. Actually, let me reconsider: with softmax temperature 1 over Pearson correlations, values cluster at ~0.02–0.04. The column is still monotone with margin. OK.\n\nAnother issue: the \"margin\" column is saved only to run_summary, not to the required CSV (good — the CSV is exactly 5 columns).\n\nNext, the report. Let me also compute additional ambiguity statistics for the report:\n- Number of rows with negative margin (runner_up beats the assigned guide): from the full mapping table: CDC73, CPSF2, CPSF3, CPSF4, CPSF6, CSTF1, CSTF3, CTR9, FIP1L1, NT, PAF1, PHF3, RPAP2, RPRD1A, RPRD1B, SCAF1, SCAF4, SCAF8, SF3A1, SYMPK, THOC5 → counting from the table: negative margins: CDC73(−0.229), CPSF2(−0.140), CPSF3(−0.093), CPSF4(−0.092), CPSF6(−0.008), CSTF1(−0.068), CSTF3(−0.065), CTR9(−0.060), FIP1L1(−0.001), NT(−0.119), PAF1(−0.101), PHF3(−0.024), RPAP2(−0.106), RPRD1A(−0.036), RPRD1B(−0.038), SCAF1(−0.088), SCAF4(−0.034), SCAF8(−0.014), SF3A1(−0.081), SYMPK(−0.076), THOC5(−0.022) = 21 rows. 21 of 34 have negative margin — substantial ambiguity under one-to-one constraints. Hmm.\n\nHmm, this bothers me. Under global one-to-one assignment, 21 of 34 rows get a guide that isn't their own best. Alternative: report per-gene argmax (no one-to-one constraint) — then each gene gets its own best query guide, multiple genes may share a guide, and margins are all ≥0. Which is more appropriate for \"map the query perturbation groups to the labeled reference\"?\n\nThe truth is a function: each query guide targets exactly one gene. So the mapping query→gene is a function (many-to-one allowed). The inverse, gene→query guides, is one-to-many (genes can have 0–2 guides). My deliverable is gene→guide (one guide per gene). Under the truth, the \"correct\" gene→guide row picks one of that gene's true guides.\n\nHungarian one-to-one forces injectivity gene→guide, which isn't part of the ground truth structure (genes can have multiple guides in the query — but here reference has 33 genes with 1 guide each, and query has 32 perturbed guides, so most genes have exactly one query guide… if query guides are a subset of the reference screen's guides). If the query is the same screen with one guide missing, the true gene→guide mapping is a bijection between 32 genes and 32 guides, with 1 gene having no guide. Under that assumption, one-to-one Hungarian is the correct constraint! And PHF3/SCAF8: one of them is the missing gene; the other should have gotten a real guide, but its true guide was stolen (by CSTF1? guide28's query-side best is CSTF1 at 0.371, but Hungarian gave guide28 to CTR9… no wait, guide28→CTR9 in the gene table (CTR9 guide28). Hmm wait: CTR9→guide28, and guide28's best gene is CSTF1 (0.371). And CSTF1→guide16 (0.468)? But guide16's best gene is SCAF1 (0.519)! Yet Hungarian gave guide16 to CSTF1. Because SCAF1 took… SCAF1→guide26 (0.432), but guide26's best is SCAF1 (0.432)? guide26 best = SCAF1 0.4319 — consistent. But guide16 also has SCAF1 best (0.519), and SCAF1 only got guide26. So Hungarian sacrificed guide16's SCAF1 match (0.519) to give CSTF1 guide16 (0.468)… that doesn't maximize the sum unless SCAF1's alternative (guide26, 0.432) vs CSTF1's alternative (guide28?, 0.371) makes the tradeoff favor it: SCAF1(0.519)+CSTF1(0.371)=0.890 vs SCAF1(0.432)+CSTF1(0.468)=0.900. Yes, the latter wins globally. OK, Hungarian did its job; this is just a degenerate, ambiguous region (SCAF1/CSTF1/CSTF3/SYMPK/CTR9/LEO1 all correlated at ~0.3–0.5).\n\nSo the results stand, but I must quantify the ambiguity clearly: 21/34 with negative margin, stability 76%, and the three genes of interest have positive margin (PABPC1 0.194, NUDT21 0.123, LEO1 0.091) — LEO1 is the weakest, with documented confusion with CTR9.\n\nHmm, wait — let me actually double-check the LEO1 situation by computing C[LEO1, guide28] and the full assignment alternative. Also check stability: in the subsample run, did LEO1 stay at guide14? Stability details are unknown (only aggregate 0.76). In the report I can state that the 3 target genes were stable (need to verify). run_summary.json only has aggregates. Let me quickly re-run the stability comparison for the 3 genes and extract per-target stability + which rows flipped. Small addition to a check script (recompute build frac=0.5 — takes ~3 s, no problem).\n\nAlso for the report: characterize the cell-type shift (ctrl_profile_corr=0.918; median diagonal signature correlation vs off-diagonal).\n\nLet me reconsider: should NT be included as a row? NT row: score 0.018, negative margin — noise, as expected (NT has no signature). Including it makes 34 rows = all reference labels. I'll keep it and note that the NT row is a control with signature ~0 by construction; its assignment (NT-I8) is arbitrary among NT guides — that's honest. Alternatively exclude NT → 33 rows. Hmm. \"Map the query perturbation groups to the labeled reference\" — labeled reference includes NT controls. Keep 34, document it.\n\nHmm, one more consideration on row count: the query has 32 perturbed guides and the reference has 33 perturbed genes → under the subset hypothesis, one gene's row has no true answer (its guide isn't in the query). Hungarian forces a guide onto it anyway — for that row, the true answer is \"absent.\" Evidence: SCAF8 (best correlation 0.054, noise) and PHF3 (0.066, noise) — two candidates for the missing gene, plus guide32 has noise-level best (0.052 → RPRD1B). Wait, guide32: if guide32 is a real perturbation guide, its signature should match something. Best is 0.052 — noise. So guide32 might target PHF3 or SCAF8 (cross-cell-type transfer failed), or guide32 is a broken guide. Interesting: three weak entities (PHF3, SCAF8, guide32). Under the subset hypothesis: one gene is missing; if guide32 targets one of {PHF3, SCAF8} but failed to transfer, the other gene is the missing one. Cannot resolve — quantify as ambiguity. Report: the weakest row is ambiguous between PHF3/SCAF8/guide32; both are assigned to NT guides (PHF3→NT-I7, SCAF8→NT-I3), meaning Hungarian found no substantive perturbation match for them.\n\nHmm wait, that means under the subset hypothesis, one of PHF3/SCAF8 should have taken guide32… Hungarian gave guide32 to RPRD1B (score 0.0516!) while PHF3 and SCAF8 got NT guides (0.042, 0.040). Total: RPRD1B(0.052)+PHF3(0.042)+SCAF8(0.040)=0.134 vs, say, RPRD1B→guide5(0.328? no wait, guide5 is assigned to RPRD1B in the gene table? RPRD1B→guide5 0.328? From the table: RPRD1B guide5 0.328119. But in the supplementary guide5 best = PCF11 0.455. Hmm, so Hungarian gave guide5 to RPRD1B?? Let me re-read: gene table: \"RPRD1B guide5 0.328119\". Supplementary: \"guide5 PCF11 0.455429, runner-up CPSF3 0.424955\" — RPRD1B isn't even guide5's runner-up. And PCF11→guide20 (0.559). So Hungarian gave guide5 (PCF11's top choice at 0.455) to RPRD1B (0.328)?? That would reduce the sum unless PCF11 got a better guide: PCF11→guide20 (0.559 vs guide20's best PCF11 0.559 — consistent). So the tradeoff: PCF11(0.559)+RPRD1B(0.328)=0.887 vs PCF11(0.455 via guide5)+RPRD1B(0.052 via guide32)=0.507. Hungarian is correct: RPRD1B's true match is probably guide5… no wait. Hmm, guide5's best gene is PCF11 (0.455); RPRD1B-guide5 = 0.328. But if guide5 truly targets RPRD1B and PCF11's true guide is guide20 (0.559 — guide20's best is PCF11), everything is consistent: PCF11→guide20 ✓, RPRD1B→guide5 (but RPRD1B's signal looks more like PCF11's — pathway similarity: RBBP6, PCF11, RPRD1B are all 3′ processing / CTD-associated; ambiguity again).\n\nOK, the structure is: strong blocks of pathway-similar signatures: CPSF complex block (CPSF1/2/3/4/6/7, FIP1L1, PAPOLA, SYMPK, PCF11, CSTF1/2/3, NUDT21, PABPC1, PABPN1, RBBP6, SCAF4/8/11/1, THOC5…), PAF1 block (PAF1, CTR9, LEO1, CDC73), RPRD1A/B, RPAP2, SRSF3, SF3A1, PHF3. Cross-dataset correlations within blocks are comparable → real ambiguity. The method captures it; the report must quantify it.\n\nDecision: keep the method, finalize the CSV (already written out), run one targeted check (per-target stability of the 3 genes under subsampling + rows that flipped + verify the CSV schema), and then write report.md. Also fix the NT sanity metric wording in the summary? The run_summary.json already written says nt_query_guides_best_hit_NT_frac=0.0, which is misleading as a \"failure\" — but it's expected behavior (NT signatures are noise). Explain in the report. Don't re-run the whole pipeline for that; the JSON is an intermediate artifact. Fine — or should I patch analysis.py to use the better sanity metric and quickly re-run (cheap, ~6 s)? The instructions say patch only what's needed; the NT metric inside analysis.py is a design flaw in the check (I asserted something that can't hold). A small patch + re-run preserves the script's validity as a deliverable. analysis.py is a required deliverable — it should be valid code with reasonable checks. Patch the NT sanity to: (a) NT row gets assigned to an NT guide; (b) the max absolute correlation of NT query guides is small (<0.15); and re-run once. That regenerates the CSV identically (the production path is deterministic, same seed) — safe.\n\nLet me also grab the per-target stability info during that same re-run: add to the summary which rows flipped and whether the 3 target genes were stable. Small patch, one re-run. Then write the report."}}
