{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_34","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\Users\\\\chenyuqiong\\\\.cache\\\\codex-runtimes\\\\codex-primary-runtime\\\\dependencies\\\\native\\\\powershell\\\\pwsh.exe\" -Command \"@'\n# Generate report.md from validated results (no rerun of the analysis)\nimport json, pandas as pd\ndf = pd.read_csv(\\\"output/guide_mapping.csv\\\")\nsup = pd.read_csv(\\\"output/supplementary_query_guide_mapping.csv\\\")\ns = json.load(open(\\\"output/run_summary.json\\\"))\n\ndf = df.sort_values(\\\"target_gene\\\")\ndf[\\\"margin\\\"] = df.score - df.runner_up_score\nneg = int((df.margin < 0).sum())\ntab1 = df.drop(columns=[\\\"margin\\\"]).to_markdown(index=False)\ntab2 = df[[\\\"target_gene\\\",\\\"query_guide_id\\\",\\\"score\\\",\\\"runner_up_score\\\",\\\"margin\\\"]].to_markdown(index=False)\n\ndef nat(x): return x.str.replace(\\\"guide\\\",\\\"\\\",regex=False).astype(int) if x.str.fullmatch(r\\\"guide\\\\d+\\\").all() else x\nsup = sup.sort_values(\\\"query_guide_id\\\", key=lambda x: [ (0,int(t.replace(\\\"guide\\\",\\\"\\\"))) if t.startswith(\\\"guide\\\") else (1,int(t.split(\\\"-I\\\")[0].replace(\\\"NT\\\",\\\"0\\\")) or 0) for t in x])\nsup2 = sup.sort_values(\\\"query_guide_id\\\", key=lambda x: pd.Series([(0,int(t.replace(\\\"guide\\\",\\\"\\\"))) if t.startswith(\\\"guide\\\") else (1, int(t.split(\\\"-I\\\")[1])) for t in x], index=x.index))\ntab3 = sup2.to_markdown(index=False)\n\ntargets = s[\\\"targets\\\"]\nreport = f\\\"\\\"\\\"# Perturb-seq query-to-reference guide mapping — report\n\n## Task\nMap the anonymized query perturbation groups (\"'`guide1..guide32`, plus `NT-I*`\nnon-targeting controls) onto the labeled reference (43 guides / 34 target genes,\none guide per perturbed gene), robust to the cell-type/state shift between the two\ndatasets, and identify the query guide IDs corresponding to **PABPC1, NUDT21, LEO1**.\n\n## Data\n| | reference | query |\n|---|---|---|\n| cells | {s['\"'n_ref_cells']} | {s['n_qry_cells']} |\n| genes (measured) | 11,889 | 11,889 |\n| shared genes used | {s['n_shared_genes']} | {s['n_shared_genes']} |\n| obs metadata | \"'`guide`, `target_gene` | `guide` only |\n| guides | 10 NT + 33 perturbed (1/gene) | 10 NT + 32 anonymized perturbed |\n| matrix | raw UMI counts | raw UMI counts |\n\nCross-dataset shift: the NT-control mean expression profiles of the two datasets\ncorrelate at r = {s['\"'ctrl_profile_corr']:.3f}, i.e. the query lives in a shifted\ncellular context. Matching is therefore performed on *perturbation effects*\n(guide mean minus own-dataset NT control mean), never on absolute expression.\n\n## Leakage prevention\n1. **No target metadata in the query.** Asserted at runtime: the query AnnData\n   \"'`obs` contains only the anonymized `guide` column; no gene/target field exists.\n2. **No name-based matching.** Guide-name strings are never compared across\n   datasets and never parsed to infer gene identity. The only label semantics used\n   is the explicit `NT` non-targeting-control designation, which is part of the\n   query'\"'s own experimental design (needed to estimate the control baseline).\n3. **Within-dataset baselines only.** Each dataset is normalized independently\n   (CPM 1e4 + log1p) and signatures are computed relative to each dataset's own NT\n   mean, so no cross-dataset baseline or cell-type composition information leaks\n   into the scores.\n4. **Label-column aggregation only.** Reference guide signatures are grouped to\n   \"'`target_gene` strictly via the `target_gene` label column.\n\n## Primary method\n1. Normalize each dataset independently (library-size 1e4, log1p).\n2. Restrict to the {s['\"'n_shared_genes']} shared genes.\n3. Perturbation signature per guide = mean(log-normalized expression) − own-dataset\n   NT-control mean (log fold-change vector).\n4. Reference signatures aggregated to \"'`target_gene` (NT = average of its 10 control\n   guides ≈ 0 by construction).\n5. Score(gene, query guide) = Pearson correlation of the two signature vectors.\n6. One-to-one assignment (reference has one guide per gene, so a bijective\n   gene→guide matching is the natural constraint) by Hungarian maximization of the\n   total score.\n7. `score` = correlation of the assigned pair; `runner_up_score` = best correlation\n   of the same gene with any *other* query guide; `confidence` = softmax probability\n   of the assigned guide over all 42 query-guide scores for that gene\n   (uniform chance level = 1/42 ≈ 0.024).\n\n## Headline result\n| target_gene | query_guide_id | score | runner_up_score | confidence |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **PABPC1** | **guide18** | {targets['\"'PABPC1']['score']:.3f} | {targets['PABPC1']['runner_up_score']:.3f} | {targets['PABPC1']['confidence']:.3f} |\n| **NUDT21** | **guide13** | {targets['NUDT21']['score']:.3f} | {targets['NUDT21']['runner_up_score']:.3f} | {targets['NUDT21']['confidence']:.3f} |\n| **LEO1** | **guide14** | {targets['LEO1']['score']:.3f} | {targets['LEO1']['runner_up_score']:.3f} | {targets['LEO1']['confidence']:.3f} |\n\n- **PABPC1 → guide18**: strongest case; guide18's own best reference hit is also\n  PABPC1 (mutually consistent), margin +{targets['PABPC1']['margin']:.3f}, stable\n  under subsampling.\n- **NUDT21 → guide13**: mutually consistent, margin +{targets['NUDT21']['margin']:.3f},\n  stable under subsampling. Main confounder is CPSF6 (same 3'-end processing\n  pathway, r = 0.513 with the query signature).\n- **LEO1 → guide14**: weakest of the three. Margin +{targets['LEO1']['margin']:.3f},\n  but from the query side guide14 scores CTR9 slightly higher (0.370 vs 0.314) —\n  CTR9 and LEO1 are both PAF1-complex subunits with near-degenerate knockdown\n  phenotypes — and the LEO1 row **flips under 50% subsampling** (see below). Treat\n  the LEO1 assignment as tentative: the data support \\\"PAF1-complex guide\\\" more\n  strongly than they distinguish LEO1 from CTR9.\n\n## Ambiguity quantification\n- **Margins**: {neg}/34 rows have negative margin (some *other* query guide\n  correlates better with the reference signature than the globally assigned one).\n  This is inherent to forcing a one-to-one assignment in a regime where several\n  genes of the same pathway (CPSF1/2/3/4/6, CSTF1/3, SCAF1, SYMPK, CTR9/LEO1/PAF1)\n  have strongly correlated knockdown signatures. Median score = {df.score.median():.3f},\n  median margin = {df.margin.median():.3f}.\n- **Subsample stability**: recomputing all signatures on a random 50% of each\n  group's cells (seed 0) and re-assigning leaves {s['subsample_stability']*100:.1f}%\n  of rows unchanged. Unstable rows: {', '.join(s['subsample_unstable_genes'])} —\n  concentrated exactly in the low-margin PAF1-complex block (CDC73, CTR9, LEO1,\n  PAF1) and the CPSF block (CPSF2, CPSF3), plus SF3A1 and PHF3.\n- **Negative-control floor**: the 10 query NT guides show only noise-level\n  correlations with all perturbation signatures (max |r| = {s['nt_guide_max_abs_corr']:.3f}),\n  and the NT row is still assigned an NT guide — the signal captured above is\n  perturbation biology, not technical artifact.\n- **Unresolvable block**: PHF3 (best r = 0.066), SCAF8 (best r = 0.054) and\n  query guide32 (best r = 0.052) all sit at noise level. The query contains 32\n  perturbed guides vs 33 reference genes, so one gene's guide is absent from the\n  query; the data cannot decide whether PHF3 or SCAF8 is the missing gene, nor\n  whether guide32 is a failed perturbation of the other. Both genes were assigned\n  residual NT guides by the global optimizer; those two rows are effectively\n  unmapped and are flagged as such.\n\n## Validation performed (lightweight, independent)\n1. 50%-subsample re-derivation of every signature and of the full assignment\n   (above) — an independent stochastic rerun of the whole pipeline, not a\n   parameter sweep.\n2. Negative-control sanity: NT↔NT assignment and noise-level NT correlations\n   (above).\n\n## Full gene → query-guide mapping (output/guide_mapping.csv)\n{tab1}\n\nMargins (score − runner_up_score):\n{tab2}\n\n## Supplementary: query-guide → best reference gene view\n(\"'`output/supplementary_query_guide_mapping.csv`; explanatory only — the required\nmachine-readable artifact is `guide_mapping.csv` with one row per reference gene)\n\n{tab3}\n\n## Limitations\n- One guide per perturbed gene in the reference: no within-dataset replicate\n  guides to estimate guide-level noise; ambiguity was therefore quantified by\n  margins, subsample stability and control floors instead.\n- Pearson correlation of mean log-fold-change signatures is a simple summary;\n  pathway-level signature similarity (CPSF complex, PAF1 complex, CSTF complex)\n  sets a hard ceiling on gene-level identifiability across the cell-type shift.\n- The LEO1 row (and the PAF1 block generally) should be interpreted as a\n  pathway-level assignment; the CSTF2/CPSF2/CPSF7/RPAP2/RPRD1A rows are likewise\n  low-confidence and their negative margins are reported in the CSV.\n\"\"\"\nopen(\"output/report.md\", \"w\", encoding=\"utf-8\").write(report)\nprint(\"report written:\", len(report), \"chars\")\n'\"'@ | Set-Content -Encoding utf8 _report.py; python _report.py\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
